# Infrastructure

This repository creates no cloud resources. {{ project_name }} runs on a shared platform that
is defined in its own repository: one host with Docker Compose, one PostgreSQL server for
every product, Caddy for TLS and S3-compatible object storage. Changes to the machine itself
are made there, never here.

Only a `dev` environment exists. `prod` is a second tenant on the same platform, added there
(database, bucket, hostname) and here (a `deploy-prod` workflow with its own GitHub
environment and `DEPLOY_ENV=prod`) once real users or a TestFlight build depend on it.

## What the platform provides

The product is known to the platform as `{{ platform_product }}` (`platform_product` in
`.copier-answers.yml`). For each environment the platform owns:

| What | Name |
|---|---|
| Directory on the host | `/opt/platform/products/{{ platform_product }}-<env>/` |
| Database and role | `{{ platform_product }}_<env>`; `DATABASE_URL` in `db.env` in that directory |
| Docker network | `platform` (external); PostgreSQL is reachable on it as `postgres:5432` |
| Hostname and TLS | Caddy site `{{ platform_product }}-<env>`, proxied to `{{ platform_product }}-<env>-api:8080` |
| Object storage | one bucket per environment, with a key scoped to it |
| Backups | nightly database dumps |

The platform repository's README says how a product is added and how to read its values
(hostname, bucket, credentials).

## What this repository does (`deploy/`)

`deploy/compose.yaml` runs only the API container, named `{{ platform_product }}-<env>-api`,
on the `platform` network, with `DATABASE_URL` loaded from `db.env`. It publishes no ports and
starts no database or proxy of its own. The `deploy-dev` workflow builds the image, pushes it
to GHCR, then over SSH as the `deploy` user writes `compose.yml` and `.env` into
`/opt/platform/products/{{ platform_product }}-dev/` and runs `docker compose pull` and
`docker compose up -d`.

First-time setup (once per product):

1. On the platform: add the `{{ platform_product }}` / `dev` tenant, provision its database
   (this writes `db.env`) and add its Caddy site.
2. On the host, as `deploy`: `docker login ghcr.io` with a token that can read packages.
3. In GitHub, `dev` environment secrets: `DEPLOY_HOST` (the platform host) and
   `DEPLOY_SSH_KEY` (the platform's CI key, which can only log in as `deploy`).

## PostgreSQL version

**PostgreSQL 18** everywhere. The platform's server, the local Devbox package
(`postgresql_18` in `devbox.json`, also used by CI) and any SQL feature the API relies on
(for example native `uuidv7()`) must be on the same major. Move to a new major only after the
platform has been upgraded, by changing the Devbox package here.

## Local development

Nothing here is needed locally: `just db-up` starts the Devbox PostgreSQL on this product's
own port (`DEV_DB_PORT`, default {{ dev_db_port }}) and the API listens on plain HTTP.
